/ `--` ends flag parsing (contributed by bleedsa): later tokens are positional,
/ so a script can receive -q, a second --, or a dash-prefixed name in .z.i.
/ Without --, flags are consumed wherever they appear (t784's exe/cmd probe).
cmd:0="%COMSPEC%"~*`4:"echo %COMSPEC%"
exe:$[cmd;"..\\gk";"../gk"]
`4:exe," -q args.k a b"
`4:exe," -q args.k -q a -q"
`4:exe," -q args.k -- -q -i 5"
`4:exe," -q args.k a -- b -q"
`4:exe," -q args.k -- --"
`4:exe," -q args.k --"
`4:exe," -q -- args.k -x"
\\
